{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Blender Interface Areas.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Blender Interface Areas.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"blender interface areas blender interface areas","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Blender Interface Areas.md","fileName":"Blender Interface Areas.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Blender Interface Areas","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-07 23:25\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[Blender Interface and Workflow]]","tags":["baby"],"links":["Blender Interface and Workflow","blenderfordummies4thedition.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Blender Interface Areas.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Blender Interface Areas.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"blender interface areas blender interface areas","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/Blender Interface Areas.md","fileName":"Blender Interface Areas.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"Blender Interface Areas","heading":"Blender Interface Areas","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"A Blender workspace can span one or more windows, and each window is divided into areas. Every area holds an editor, while regions such as a header, Toolbar, or Sidebar provide controls belonging to that editor.\n\nAreas share a common layout system regardless of editor type. Their borders can be dragged to resize them, and corner widgets or border commands can split one area into two or join neighboring areas. This lets a workspace be adjusted without opening overlapping dialogs.","tags":["baby"],"links":["Blender Interface and Workflow","blenderfordummies4thedition.pdf"],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/Blender Interface Areas.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/Blender Interface Areas.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKe